{"name":"Golden Staff","hex":"#f7eb83","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"golden","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Made of, or relating to, gold.","Having a color or other richness suggestive of gold."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["(Can we verify this sense?) brassy drummer, brassy chub, etc. (Kyphosus vaigiensis), a fish found in southeast Asia.","Ellipsis of golden retriever."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/golden"},{"term":"staff","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A long, straight, thick wooden rod or stick, especially one used to assist in walking.","A series of horizontal lines on which musical notes are written; a stave."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To supply (a business, volunteer organization, etc.) with employees or staff members."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/staff"}],"entries":[{"title":"Staphylococcus aureus","blocks":[{"text":"Staphylococcus aureus is a gram-positive spherically shaped bacterium, a member of the Bacillota, and is a usual member of the microbiota of the body, frequently found in the upper respiratory tract and on the skin. It is often positive for catalase and nitrate reduction and is a facultative anaerobe, meaning that it can grow without oxygen. Although S. aureus usually acts as a commensal of the human microbiota, it can also become an opportunistic pathogen, being a common cause of skin infections including abscesses, respiratory infections such as sinusitis, and food poisoning. Pathogenic strains often promote infections by producing virulence factors such as potent protein toxins, and the expression of a cell-surface protein that binds and inactivates antibodies. S. aureus is one of the leading pathogens for deaths associated with antimicrobial resistance and the emergence of antibiotic-resistant strains, such as methicillin-resistant S. aureus (MRSA). The bacterium is a worldwide problem in clinical medicine. Despite much research and development, no vaccine for S. aureus has been approved."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d3/Staphylococcus_aureus_VISA_2.jpg/330px-Staphylococcus_aureus_VISA_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Staphylococcus_aureus","wikidata":"Q188121"},{"title":"Gold (color)","blocks":[{"text":"Gold, also called golden, is a color tone resembling the chemical element gold."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5b/GoldNuggetUSGOV.jpg/330px-GoldNuggetUSGOV.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gold_(color)","wikidata":"Q208045"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/08/Staphylococcus_aureus_appearance_on_agar_plates.jpg/500px-Staphylococcus_aureus_appearance_on_agar_plates.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/08/Staphylococcus_aureus_appearance_on_agar_plates.jpg/1280px-Staphylococcus_aureus_appearance_on_agar_plates.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/08/Staphylococcus_aureus_appearance_on_agar_plates.jpg/500px-Staphylococcus_aureus_appearance_on_agar_plates.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Staphylococcus aureus on basic cultivation media","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AStaphylococcus_aureus_appearance_on_agar_plates.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/51/Staphylococcus_aureus_identification.jpg/500px-Staphylococcus_aureus_identification.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/51/Staphylococcus_aureus_identification.jpg/1280px-Staphylococcus_aureus_ide